# 7. The Utility Compiler

Implement a genuine compiler pipeline.

Conceptual flow:

```text
Configuration
     ↓
Content discovery
     ↓
Candidate extraction
     ↓
Candidate normalization
     ↓
Utility and variant parsing
     ↓
Theme resolution
     ↓
CSS rule generation
     ↓
Rule deduplication and ordering
     ↓
Output optimization
     ↓
Compiled CSS
```

Keep candidate parsing independent from utility generation.

The compiler must distinguish:

- Known utilities
- Known utility families with invalid values
- Known variants
- Unsupported variants
- Arbitrary values
- Explicitly safelisted classes
- Custom registered utilities
- Invalid or ambiguous candidates

When a class is unsupported, offer a useful diagnostic in development mode. Do not output broken CSS silently.

## Important compiler properties

- Correct CSS selector escaping
- Correct handling of special characters
- Safe arbitrary-value parsing
- Stable CSS ordering
- Deduplication
- Deterministic output
- Repeatable build results
- Source-size and candidate-count limits
- Useful diagnostic messages
- No execution of source-code expressions
- No use of `eval()` or `new Function()` to interpret class names

Only generate CSS for recognized utilities. Never allow a class name to inject raw declarations into a rule without controlled parsing and validation.

