Variants allow utilities to be applied conditionally.

Implement a useful initial variant system.

## Responsive variants

Provide configurable breakpoint variants such as:

- `sm:`
- `md:`
- `lg:`
- `xl:`
- `2xl:`

Example:

```html
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4"></div>
```

Breakpoints must be configurable, ordered predictably, and applied without duplicating identical CSS rules.

Document whether a breakpoint means a minimum width or another condition.

## Interactive pseudo-class variants

Implement useful variants such as:

- `hover:`
- `focus:`
- `focus-visible:`
- `active:`
- `visited:`
- `disabled:`
- `checked:`
- `first:`
- `last:`
- `odd:`
- `even:`
- `required:`
- `invalid:`

Only advertise variants that are implemented correctly.

## Dark mode

Support configurable dark-mode strategies, including a system-preference strategy and a class or data-attribute strategy.

Example:

```html
<div class="bg-background text-foreground dark:bg-dark-background dark:text-dark-foreground"></div>
```

Do not hard-code the example token names unless the theme defines them.

The dark-mode selector must be documented. Ensure generated selectors actually work with the chosen strategy.

## Group and peer variants

Implement useful parent-child interaction patterns after basic variants are stable.

Example:

```html
<a class="group inline-flex items-center gap-2">
  <span>Explore</span>
  <span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1">
