# 10. Arbitrary Values and Custom Utilities

One of the defining advantages of utility-first CSS is its ability to represent one-off design requirements without forcing the developer to define a new global class.

Support a controlled arbitrary-value grammar such as:

```html
<div class="w-[37rem]"></div>
<div class="grid-cols-[auto_1fr_auto]"></div>
<div class="top-[calc(100%+1rem)]"></div>
<div class="bg-[#101014]"></div>
<div class="text-[clamp(2rem,5vw,5rem)]"></div>
```

The above are illustrative. Confirm each form works in the actual parser and generated CSS.

Requirements:

- Parse bracket contents using balanced delimiters.
- Preserve meaningful nested expressions.
- Convert documented underscore conventions when needed.
- Validate property values where a practical allowlist exists.
- Escape the class selector correctly.
- Avoid treating arbitrary strings as raw CSS declarations.
- Reject malformed candidates with understandable errors.

Implement an extensible utility registry and a custom utility mechanism.

For example, a project should be able to define a custom utility or token through its supported configuration system without patching OrayCSS's source files.

Custom utilities must participate in normal candidate extraction, ordering, variants, deduplication, and build output.

Do not expose unsafe plugin execution or dynamically evaluate arbitrary strings from content files.

